p><h2><span style=3D"background-color: transparent;">Four labs, one experim=
ent, four different answers</span></h2><p>Researchers at four major univers=
ities set out to run the exact same chemistry experiment: using a special c=
hemical reaction to help make synthetic fuel.</p><p>Despite following the s=
ame recipe, all four labs got completely different outcomes. The surprising=
 culprit? How vigorously each team stirred the liquid.</p><p>Stirring chang=
ed the physical environment of the reaction, meaning the labs weren't actua=
lly running the exact same test after all. One team's "normal stir" was ano=
ther team's "vigorous shake," and that unwritten habit created huge gaps in=
 their data.</p><p>When scientists upload this data to train an AI, the AI =
doesn't know stirring speed was the missing variable. It just sees four lab=
s running the "same" experiment with four contradictory outcomes. Instead o=
f learning real chemistry, the AI learns useless noise.</p><p>The study was=
